﻿@* ----------------------------------------------------------------
    UnifiedAlertAnalysis view -- component version (Major.Minor.Build.Revision)
                Version: 1.0.3.4

    1.0.3.4 - TREND IS GONE. THE DAY VIEW CARRIES THE CAUSE CODES INSTEAD.

        THE TREND VIEW IS REMOVED ENTIRELY (owner). The fourth segment, the waveform,
        the cause-code small multiples, the Overall/By cause code/By day split control
        and every AA_MX_TREND_* constant and .aa-mxt-/.aa-mxs- rule are deleted -- about
        1,070 lines across markup, script and CSS. The 1.0.3.0 and 1.0.3.1 notes below
        describe a view that no longer exists; they are left in place because they record
        WHY the calendar-day bucketing was wrong, and that reasoning still applies to any
        future chart on this data. Nothing else referenced those functions. A dead
        .aa-mxr rule from a long-removed risk map went with them.

        EVERY DAY WITH DATA. AA_MX_DAY_CAP kept the newest fourteen and printed "N earlier
        days not shown -- narrow the date window to reach them", which asks the operator
        to run a second query for data the first one already returned. The cap is gone. A
        day with no scored alerts still gets no row: "no alerts" and "no agreement" are
        different facts and a zero-height bar would assert the second.

        THE CAUSE CODES ARE NOW UNDER EACH DAY. The bar said a day went badly and never
        said what went badly; finding out meant switching to the cause view, which
        averages the whole window flat and so cannot tell you it was that day. Every code
        on the day is chipped -- not only the ones behind disagreements, because twenty
        alerts of one code is a story whether the AI got them right or not -- with the
        day's count on each and a red badge carrying how many it got wrong, drawn only
        when there are any. The chips are BUTTONS, like the cause view's rows and unlike
        the day bar itself: a cause filter exists on this board, a day filter does not.

        DETAIL PANEL: THE ASSET'S ALERTS BY DATE, and the panel widened 720 -> 880px to
        hold the content 1.0.3.3 added. asset_history carries a 30-day total and a
        per-cause split and NO DATES, so "9 alerts" could be nine in one afternoon or
        nine across a month -- opposite findings. The dates are not in that payload, so
        they are derived from AA_ALERTS, which the board already holds, and are captioned
        as THE BOARD'S WINDOW rather than merged into the 30-day figures above them: two
        different windows on one panel have to say which is which, or the smaller one
        reads as a correction of the larger. Dates carrying THIS alert's cause code are
        marked, since that is the recurrence worth finding.
        Matched on asset AND STATION. Asset names are only unique within a station --
        there is a PT-1 at most of them -- so matching on name alone folds every
        station's PT-1 into one history and inflates every count on the strip.

    1.0.3.3 - THE DETAIL PANEL SHOWS THE CASE, NOT THE BUILD. Pairs with controller
        1.0.1.3, which stopped projecting the build metadata and started projecting the
        analysis content that was already in the payload and being discarded.

        WHAT IT USED TO BE. One full card per analysis ROW, each ending in eleven lines
        of key/value build metadata -- engine, provider, model, turns, tokens, est. cost,
        model time, total time, verification tier, server node, controller version. On a
        real alert that is six near-identical cards and sixty-six lines of metadata, and
        not one of those lines changes what an operator does about a point machine.

        MOST ROWS ARE NOT ANALYSES. A row carrying served_from_cache is a REPLAY of an
        earlier verdict: no model call, no new evidence, the same text served again. On
        the alert this was built against, four of six rows are replays. Six equal cards
        told the reader the alert had been analysed six times, which was false. The panel
        now leads with the newest REAL analysis in full, and every run -- replays included
        and labelled as replays -- goes in one compact strip at the foot.

        WHEN TWO REAL ANALYSES DISAGREE, IT SAYS SO, AND USUALLY WHY. That alert holds a
        NOT_CONFIRMED ("transient, no action") and a later CONFIRMED ("Field HW, inspect
        the circuit"). Showing only the newest buries a reversal; showing both silently
        leaves an operator to choose between them with nothing to choose on. The banner
        names the split, and where the earlier run was made before the alert reset -- the
        case here, and visible in the payload as a run whose input carried no reset -- it
        says that, because it is the entire explanation. Two clocks are kept apart there
        deliberately: when the earlier run FINISHED, and when the alert actually reset.

        WHAT IS ON THE PANEL NOW, in reading order: verdict, confidence and category;
        headline; likely cause; fired -> reset with the duration implied; THE MEASUREMENT
        (the reading, its threshold, its normal, the change) -- the one section whose
        absence made everything above it unverifiable; what fired, against which limits,
        PASS/FAIL each; the relay states either side of the event, with the one that
        CHANGED marked, because on this alert NWKR DROP -> PICKUP is the whole recovery;
        evidence with its provenance as a chip rather than four literal characters at the
        head of the sentence; recommended action; caveats; the asset's 30-day history with
        sameCause called out separately; and the live readings, collapsed, sorted so
        out-of-band values come first, with the count of them in the summary.

        Nothing here is new data. All of it was arriving in the payload and being thrown
        away one layer earlier.

    1.0.3.2 - TABLE: DENSITY AND FINISH. Restyle only -- same columns, same data,
        same behaviour. Every rule is scoped to .aa-tbl, because the disc, the rating
        chip, the validity switch, INSIGHT and Detail are SHARED with the cards and the
        timeline, where they are the primary surface and are correctly sized already.

        MEASURED FIRST. Data rows were 67-75px and the header 55px, so a 900px viewport
        held about eight alerts. The height was not padding: .aa-cause-in is a
        two-column grid holding THREE children -- the code, INSIGHT and Detail -- so
        Detail wrapped onto a second grid row and every row in the table paid ~25px for
        it. The cause cell measured 49px against the AI cell's 33px. Laying the three
        out with flex instead drops it to 24px. With that gone, a 46px row height (a
        MINIMUM under table-layout:fixed) sets the rhythm, and rows are now 46-47px --
        about 32% shorter and, more usefully, uniform: the old 8px row-to-row variance
        was the thing that made a wide table tiring to scan.

        Putting the buttons on the code's line takes width from the code, and the cause
        code is the row's identifier, so the buttons give width back (the word INSIGHT
        does not need letter-spacing at 10px) and the column takes ~34px from the remark
        columns, which clamp to two lines and carry their full text on the title either
        way. The 11-column total is unchanged.

        BANDING is the one thing an eleven-column row most needed -- something to hold
        the line from SR across to the last remark. It is on the CELL, not the row: the
        severity rail and the column hairlines are inset shadows on cells, and a row
        background sits underneath a cell's own. Parity flips at each station band,
        which is right -- the banding restarts where the reading does. Hover is on the
        cell for the same reason, and is stronger than the old #f7f9fc, which on a table
        this wide could be mistaken for a band.

        The header was bottom-aligned around a two-line label, so every single-line
        heading floated at the foot of 55px with dead space above it; centred, it reads
        as one band. It gains a shadow so a sticky header sits ON the rows it scrolls
        over rather than blending into them. The column hairlines drop to a fifth of
        their weight now that banding does the row tracking -- at equal weight with the
        row rules they turned eleven columns into a spreadsheet grid.

        INSIGHT was a filled indigo pill on all twenty-five rows, making the loudest
        thing on the table an action rather than the data. It is available on every row,
        so it is not a call to action: quiet at rest, taking its fill back on hover and
        focus. The .busy state stays loud -- it is the only signal that the click
        landed. Unchanged on the cards, where one card is one alert.

        The serial column is sticky at the left edge. Below ~1300px the shell scrolls
        sideways, and the serial cell carries the decision disc -- the anchor every
        other cell in the row is read against.

        Lining figures on the serial, the timing stack and the group counts: without
        them a column of numbers wanders a pixel or two per digit, which is exactly the
        wobble that makes it hard to read down.

    1.0.3.1 - THE WAVEFORM CAN NOW BE SPLIT BY CAUSE CODE AND BY DATE.

        A second segmented control appears on the Trend view only: Overall / By cause
        code / By day. It is a second row rather than a fourth view segment because view
        and split answer different questions -- WHAT is plotted versus HOW it is cut --
        and folding them into one strip of seven buttons would hide that the three split
        options exist only while Trend is selected.

        BY CAUSE CODE is a stack of compact waves, one per code, worst first on the same
        ranking the Cause bar view uses. The bar view answers "which faults is the AI
        wrong about" by averaging the whole window flat, so a code that was fine all
        fortnight and collapsed on Tuesday looks identical to one that has been mediocre
        throughout. These rows put the two apart.

        EVERY ROW IS MEASURED AGAINST THE SAME LINE -- the window average across all
        codes, at the same height in every row. Drawing each code against its OWN mean
        would make every row half green and half red and destroy the comparison the stack
        exists for; a code that is always bad should read as a row of red, and now does.
        Each code's own average is printed on the right instead.

        The time axis is shared and drawn once underneath, and every row uses the same
        bucket boundaries, so a dip in the same column of two rows is the same hour. An
        interval where a code had nothing scored has no point: the line spans it and the
        dots say where real samples are. The rows are BUTTONS, like the cause bars and
        unlike the day rows -- a cause filter exists on this board, so filtering to the
        code whose row just dipped is a promise the page can keep. In this split the
        cause filter is IGNORED when gathering the data, exactly as the cause bar view
        does it: keeping it would leave a one-row stack and no way back to the picture
        the row was picked out of.

        BY DAY pins the columns to dates -- the Day bar view's own cut, as a wave. It is
        the same renderer as Overall with the rung forced, so the two cannot disagree.
        Note that pinning to dates is precisely what made the 1.0.2.9 chart a straight
        line on a 24 h window; the split is the operator's to choose so it is not
        overridden, but under five columns the chart now says so underneath rather than
        letting a segment be read as a trend.

        The Outcome, Cause code and Day BAR views are untouched. Their rows are the
        filter surface (and the day rows deliberately are not), and a wave does not carry
        that as well as a bar with a number on the end of it.

    1.0.3.0 - THE TREND WAS DRAWING A STRAIGHT LINE, AND IT IS NOW A WAVEFORM.

        WHAT WAS WRONG. 1.0.2.9 grouped the trend by CALENDAR DAY. The board's default
        window is 24 h, a 24 h window straddles exactly two dates, and so on a slice of
        319 alerts the chart drew TWO points joined by one diagonal. It was not a trend,
        it was a line segment, and because the left-hand point was often a single alert
        scored at 100% it read as a dramatic collapse that had not happened.

        THE COLUMN WIDTH IS NOW CHOSEN FROM THE DATA. AA_MX_TREND_STEPS is a ladder from
        15 minutes to a week; the span of the scored rows and how many there are together
        pick the rung, aiming for about three scored alerts per column. The same 24 h
        window now draws three-hourly columns and actually rises and falls. Nothing is
        resampled or interpolated -- a column is a real count over a real interval. Rows
        with no timestamp cannot sit on a time axis at all, so they are counted and named
        under the chart instead of being dropped silently or parked at epoch zero.

        THE AVERAGE LINE IS THE WAVE'S ZERO. The area between the curve and the window
        average is filled GREEN above and RED below. "Better than usual" and "worse than
        usual" become a glance instead of an arithmetic comparison against a dashed line,
        which is the up-and-down a flat single line could never show.

        THE CURVE IS A MONOTONE CUBIC (Fritsch-Carlson), not a plain spline. A plain
        spline overshoots between samples and would draw peaks the data does not contain
        -- on a pinned 0-100 axis it can even leave the plot. Monotone interpolation
        cannot exceed the samples that bound it, so the wave is smooth and still true.
        The sampled points stay drawn on top, so the smoothing hides nothing.

        VOLUME BARS UNDER THE WAVE carry the count behind each column. A rate is only as
        good as its denominator, and the bars are what stop a 0% column built from one
        alert reading with the same weight as a 0% column built from thirty.

        THE VIEWBOX IS WIDER (1200, was 720). Stretched across a ~1400px card the old box
        scaled every glyph by about 2x, which is why the axis dates rendered enormous and
        the last one ran off the right edge; the end labels now anchor inward as well.
        The two labels that print over the fill carry a white halo. Sub-day columns get
        a day divider and a date band, so an hourly chart still says which day it is on.

        Y STAYS PINNED 0-100 and is still never auto-scaled -- fitting it to the data
        turns a wobble into a cliff and changes the chart's shape on every reload. The
        deviation fill is what makes small movement legible without inflating it.

    1.0.2.9 - THE AI AXIS NOW READS A BIT ON THE APPROVED SIDE, AND A FOURTH VIEW.

        SCORING. Approved alerts are scored from AiConfirmed (1 confirmed, 0 not);
        discarded ones still from AiVerdict text, because PendingAlertApproval has no
        such bit. Parsing a sentence on both sides was recovering an answer that was
        already stored as a boolean on one of them.

        An EMPTY bit scores as NOT ANALYSED, never as "not confirmed". Counting it as a
        negative would move every row not yet backfilled into the miss column: the
        agreement rate collapses the day this ships and it reads as the model breaking
        rather than as a column not being populated. The panel already counts and names
        unscored rows in its footnote, which is where they belong.

        AiConfirmed is read BY NAME off FRSAlert, then AlertAudit, then the matched
        PendingAlertApproval row -- the 1.0.0.4 pattern. It compiles against today's
        EDMX and starts working the moment any one of those projects the column.

               TREND, a fourth view on the same segmented control. The day view groups by date
        but stacks four counts per row, so reading a DIRECTION out of it means comparing
        fourteen bar compositions by eye. A line puts direction first, and direction is
        the only thing on this board that catches a regression -- a model swap or a
        threshold edit shows as the rate falling on a date, and Outcome and Cause both
        average the window flat.

        Oldest on the LEFT, opposite to the day view: a trend drawn backwards reads as a
        recovery when it is a decline. The y axis is pinned 0-100 and never auto-scaled --
        fitting it to the data turns a wobble into a cliff and changes the chart's shape
        on every reload. A day where nothing scored has no point rather than a zero. A red
        dot names any day 10+ points under the average and prints its figure; a hollow one
        marks a day whose rate rests on fewer than five alerts.

    1.0.2.8 - "NOT ANALYSED" WAS A BARE DASH, AND DETAIL OPENED AN EMPTY MODAL FOR IT.
        The verdict chip had three states and only two of them said anything: a green
        AI, an amber AI, and an en dash in a 26px box. On a board where a muted dash
        in every other column means "no remark recorded", that dash read as a missing
        VALUE, not a missing ANALYSIS -- and it sat two columns from a Detail button
        that spent a round trip to say "no analysis runs recorded". A dead end reached
        by clicking, discovered one alert at a time.

          valid     AI      green solid     a current analysis is stored
          outdated  AI      amber solid     one is stored but superseded
          none      NO AI   dashed outline  Insight has never run on this alert
          unknown   ...     dashed muted    the batched read has not answered yet

        UNKNOWN is a real state, not a cosmetic one. Both batched reads land after the
        first paint, so between paint and response every row looks unanalysed; NO AI
        there would be a confident wrong answer, and it is the state that would grey
        out a button about to become usable. Nothing is disabled until the answer is
        known (AA_ANALYSIS_READY), and if AlertRatings fails it never becomes known --
        so the failure mode is "everything stays clickable", never "everything dead".

        Detail is unavailable-but-hoverable rather than `disabled`. A disabled button
        does not reliably fire a tooltip, and the tooltip is the whole point: the
        operator has to find out WHY it is dead without clicking it. Same contract the
        validity switch already keeps in its own `na` state.

    1.0.2.7 - THE FIELD REMARK COLUMN IS GONE AND THE FLYOUT NAMING IS SETTLED.
        1.0.2.5 flagged that the decision flyout called a.approvalRemark "Field remark"
        while the table called the SAME field "Approved Remark", and gave "Field" to
        a.maintRemark. Two names for one field on one screen. Settled the way the table
        already had it: the flyout label is now "Approved remark", and the separate
        Field Remark column (a.maintRemark) is removed -- Railway Remark carries what
        the office recorded and was already on screen beside it.

          APPROVED  -> Approved Remark, Railway Remark   (10 columns)
          DISCARDED -> Discard Remark                    ( 9 columns)
          ALL       -> Approved, Railway, Discard        (11 columns)

    1.0.2.6 - THE REMARK COLUMNS APPEAR AND DISAPPEAR WITH THE REVIEW STATE.
        1.0.2.5 showed all four always and put an em dash in the ones that could not
        apply. On an Approved-only board that is a permanently empty Discard column
        holding 124px; on a Discarded-only board it is three of them holding 372px --
        a third of the table's width spent on cells that CANNOT have content.

          APPROVED  -> Approved Remark, Railway Remark, Field Remark   (11 columns)
          DISCARDED -> Discard Remark                                  ( 9 columns)
          ALL       -> all four                                        (12 columns)

        ALL keeps all four because both kinds of row are on screen and each still needs
        its own column -- that is the one view where the em dash earns its place.

        ONE definition (aaRemarkCols) drives the colgroup, the header, the cells and the
        colspan, and fnAaRenderTableHead runs FIRST in fnAaRenderTable so the head can
        never be a render behind the body -- including on the empty-list path, which
        returns early and would otherwise keep a stale header forever. Four places having
        to agree on a column count is the easiest thing here to get wrong, and a table
        whose head says 12 and whose body writes 10 does not read as a filter bug, it
        reads as the board being broken.

    1.0.2.5 - DAY is the third cut of the analytics, and the remark columns split by who
        wrote them.

        DAY VIEW. Outcome says how often the AI is right, Cause code says which faults it
        is wrong about, Day says WHEN -- and that is the one that catches a regression. A
        model swap, a prompt change or a threshold edit shows up as the agreement rate
        falling on a date, and neither other view can surface it because both average the
        window flat. Each day row carries the cause codes behind its disagreements on the
        tooltip, so "the 14th went bad" leads straight to "...and it was all PT R OBS"
        without switching views and re-filtering.
        Day rows are READ-ONLY. The board has no day filter -- the date window is a server
        reload, not a client filter -- so a pressable-looking row would promise something
        that cannot happen.

        FOUR REMARK COLUMNS, and a row fills only the ones that apply to it:
          APPROVED  -> Approved, Railway, Field. Three people wrote them, answering three
                       different questions; two columns was hiding two of the three.
          DISCARDED -> the discard reason, and nothing else.
        Each column reads ONE field and says "no remark" when that field is empty; the
        cells on the wrong side of the decision show an em dash instead, because "does
        not apply" and "nobody wrote one" are different facts. One accent colour per
        source on the left border, so the four are separable once the header scrolls out
        of view. Width 150 -> 124px each, or four columns would have added 300px to a
        table already at its budget.

        NOTE FOR WHOEVER READS THIS NEXT: the decision flyout (aaPopDecHtml) labels
        a.approvalRemark as "Field remark", while this table labels it "Approved Remark"
        and gives "Field" to a.maintRemark. The two namings predate this revision and now
        sit side by side on one screen. Worth settling -- but it is a data-ownership
        question, not a rendering one, so it is flagged rather than guessed at here.

    1.0.2.4 - THE SAME ANALYTICS, BY CAUSE CODE. A segmented toggle on the panel header
        switches the AI-vs-outcome counts between two questions:
          OUTCOME    -- the 2x2 over the whole slice. "How often is the AI right here."
          CAUSE CODE -- the same four counts split per cause, worst first. "WHICH faults
                        is it wrong about."
        The second is the actionable one: a 14% disagreement rate spread evenly across
        forty causes is a tuning problem, and the same rate concentrated in one cause
        code is a rule that needs fixing. A single overall percentage cannot tell those
        apart, which is why the split is a view and not a footnote.

        Each cause row carries TWO encodings, deliberately. Bar LENGTH is volume (scored
        alerts for that cause); the SEGMENTS are the outcome split. A short all-red bar
        and a long mostly-green one are different problems and this is the only view on
        the board that separates them. The disagreement count rides on every row -- shown
        in green when it is zero rather than hidden, because "this cause never disagreed"
        is the answer somebody came for.

        Rows sort by disagreements, then volume, then name, so the order is stable across
        reloads instead of shuffling. Capped at 10 with the remainder named.

        Clicking a cause row reuses fnAaSetCause -- the SAME setter the cause chips use,
        so there is one place that decides what selecting a cause means. In cause view
        the panel also ignores the cause filter when counting (fnAaFiltered's new
        ignoreCause), or picking a cause would leave a one-row chart and no way to see
        the comparison you picked it from.

    1.0.2.3 - AI VERDICT vs OUTCOME replaces the PULSE. The pulse plotted alerts per
        hour -- a shape the date window and the timeline already show twice over, and
        nothing anyone acted on. In its place, the one question this board exists to
        answer and nothing else on it showed: when a human and the AI both judged an
        alert, did they AGREE, and when they did not, WHICH WAY did it go.

        Four counts, never collapsed into one "wrong" number, because a false alert the
        AI confirmed and a true alert the AI missed are different failures with
        different fixes:
          True alert  / AI confirmed      -- agreement
          True alert  / AI not confirmed  -- the AI missed a real one
          False alert / AI confirmed      -- the AI backed a false one
          False alert / AI not confirmed  -- agreement

        GROUND TRUTH: APPROVED = a true alert (the office kept it). DISCARDED = false,
        because that is what discarding means -- EXCEPT where the field recorded
        DiscardWasValid = 1, which outranks the discard: the alert was genuine and
        throwing it away was the mistake.

        Two things are deliberately NOT hidden. An alert nobody analysed has no verdict,
        and that is not "did not confirm" -- silence is not a judgement, so those are
        excluded from the four cells and counted separately, or the agreement figure
        would improve every time somebody skipped Insight. And a discarded alert with no
        field check is counted false on the discard alone, which is weaker evidence than
        a field confirmation, so the panel says how many rest on it.

        Each row filters the board to its own alerts; clicking the active row clears it.
        The panel counts the same slice the board shows (station, cause and type applied)
        but ignores its own row filter, so clicking one does not collapse the others to
        zero under the pointer.

    1.0.2.2 - ANALYSIS DETAIL. A "Detail" button on every alert -- table row and card --
        opens a modal listing every AI analysis RUN recorded for that alert, read from
        AIAnalysis/Get?AlertId={id}&IncludeJson=1 via AlertAnalysisDetail. The AlertId
        passed is the board's own row id, the same one the analyze flow sends, so no
        AnalysisId lookup is needed.

        Each run shows the mandatory facts: the verdict and its confidence, when it was
        analysed (started/completed), the headline and likely cause, the evidence, the
        recommended action and caveats, plus engine/provider/model, who ran it, status,
        duration, tokens and cost. Newest run first, the latest flagged. Read-only, and
        a third thing from the star rating (a judgement of the analysis) and the validity
        switch (a judgement of the alert): this is what the AI actually SAID, and when.

    1.0.2.1 - WHO MARKED IT, IN WORDS. The switch tooltip said "Marked VALID by 1134" --
        the value AssessedBy actually stores, and useless to the person reading it. The
        server now resolves that id through the FRS User API and sends byName beside the
        raw value; the tooltip shows the name and falls back to the id when a lookup
        returns nothing, because "by" followed by nothing reads as an unattributed
        judgement.

        The STORED value does not change. An id survives a rename and two people can
        share a name, so the record keeps the id and only the screen gets the name.

    1.0.2.0 - WAS THE ALERT VALID? A switch on every alert, in the table and on the
        cards, writing through api/AIAssessment/Upsert as AssessmentRole REVIEWER with
        AssessedBy = the LOGIN USER ID. This is the judgement 1.0.55.101 removed: the
        old validity buttons posted to an endpoint that no longer existed, and the rule
        that came out of it -- a control that cannot save is worse than no control --
        is why this one shows nothing as settled until the server says the row was
        written, and says "Failed" on the switch itself when it was not.

        - NOT the star rating. The stars say what a person thought of the AI's
          ANALYSIS; this says whether the ALERT was worth raising. Two questions, two
          API surfaces, two controls. Sharing one chip would make an answer to either
          unreadable.
        - THE CURRENT MARK IS READ FIRST. AlertRatings now returns an `assessments`
          map beside `ratings` on the same board-load request, gathered in the same
          parallel pass, so a switch opens showing what a reviewer already recorded
          rather than inviting a second, contradicting answer.
        - THREE states, not two. WasAlertUseful is nullable, so "nobody has judged
          this" (dashed track, "Mark") is drawn differently from "a reviewer said no"
          (red, "Not valid"). Reading null as false would put a rejection on screen
          that nobody made.
        - Unmarked goes to VALID on the first click, and clicks alternate after that.
          The endpoint is an Upsert, so a mis-click is correctable rather than
          permanent.
        - An alert with no analysis run has nothing to attach a judgement to, so its
          switch is disabled and says why -- the same rule, and the same wording, as
          the star chip.
        - Timeline view is unchanged: its cards cluster several alerts into one node
          and the whole node is already a click target, so a per-alert switch has
          nowhere honest to sit there.

    1.0.1.0 - TRIAGE LAYOUT. The board answered "here are 344 rows sorted by time"; the
        question an operator actually arrives with is "what needs me next". Six stacked
        control rows cost about 600px before the first card and every one of them
        scrolled away, so filtering alert 300 meant scrolling back to the top.

        - PULSE. One bar per hour (per 5 min under a 3 h window, per day over two days),
          failure on the baseline with predictive above so failure height is comparable
          bar to bar. Click a bar to narrow the WINDOW onto it -- a reload, not a filter,
          so the counts and the pulse cannot disagree afterwards.
        - STICKY COMMAND BAR. All six rows collapse into one that follows you down the
          page. It takes its shadow only once actually stuck, via an IntersectionObserver
          probe, so a short board has no panel floating over nothing.
        - PRIORITY ORDER, default. Open failures, open predictive, then cleared by
          recency, under "Still open" / "Cleared" headings. Newest is one click away and
          restores the date groups. Time order alone buried a live 03:00 failure under
          forty cleared predictives from lunchtime.
        - REPEAT DETECTION. Same asset AND same cause more than once in the window gets
          an x-N badge; clicking narrows to those rows. On a signalling board that is not
          N alerts, it is one fault re-presenting, and it is the most actionable thing
          the data says. Keyed on asset+cause because one point machine throwing two
          different causes is two problems.
        - OPEN NOW counter, and open cards carry a live dot and a growing "3h 5m &
          counting". An unreset alert is not a moment, it is a duration still running.
        - Site tiles collapsed behind a toggle. They are reference, not workflow.

        Table, Timeline, the AI popup, the trend charts, live events and the wisdom
        panel are untouched.

    1.0.0.4 - Carries DiscardWasValid and AiVerdict, now that PendingAlertApproval is
        selecting them. The discard flyout shows the reason with a VALID / NOT VALID
        tag beside it -- "the field looked and the alert was real, but it was discarded
        anyway" is a different and more interesting fact than a bare rejection, and it
        is the closest thing to ground truth this system holds.
        AiVerdict (the AI's own CONFIRMED/etc conclusion) rides along on the AI flyout,
        clearly separated from the star rating: the verdict is what the AI concluded,
        the stars are what a person thought of that conclusion.

    1.0.0.3 - Discarded cards showed no time, no duration and no reset. The cause was
        server-side (the pending source has no SetTimeStamp column, so every one of those
        rows arrived with an empty timeSort), but two things here helped it hide:
          - aaDayLabel returned '' for an unparseable timestamp, so every affected row
            collapsed into one nameless day group instead of standing out. It now says
            "Undated".
          - aaDayLabel and aaTimeLabel both fell back to a.time, which this view has never
            populated -- the row field is timeText. So the fallback silently did nothing.
            Both now fall back to timeText, and aaTimeLabel says "no timestamp" rather
            than rendering an empty span that reads as a rendering glitch. A blank there
            is a DATA fault and should look like one.

    1.0.0.2 - The state no longer depends on an AlertAudit row, so the 1.0.0.1 dashed
        "Not reviewed" disc is gone: APPROVED now means "a row Alert Analysis shows" and
        DISCARDED means "a row Pending Alert Analysis shows", decided by which service
        returned it. AuditMatched is still carried, but it now only tells the flyout
        whether a REMARK could exist -- an approved alert with no audit row says so
        rather than claiming none was written.

    1.0.0.1 - Rows carry AuditMatched, so a missing audit row and a recorded rejection
        stop being indistinguishable on the wire.

    A NEW page beside the existing two. Views/AlertAnalysis/Index.cshtml and
    Views/PendingAlertAnalysis/Index.cshtml are UNTOUCHED and keep their own menu
    entries. This view is forked from the PendingAlertAnalysis view 1.0.55.112 and
    adds the merge on top; its own version starts at 1.0.0.0 because it is a new
    shippable component, and inheriting 1.0.55.112 would claim a history it does not
    have. The inherited history is kept below the fold so the reasoning behind the
    board, the popup and the charts is not lost.

    1.0.0.0 - ONE BOARD, TWO REVIEW STATES. Approved and Discarded. An alert awaiting
        review IS discarded as far as this page is concerned, which is why there is no
        third state and why the pending list folded in rather than becoming one.
        Default view is CARDS. Each card carries an A or D disc at its top-left.
        Severity keeps the left border and the chip: decision and severity are
        different facts and must not share a channel, or a discarded FAILURE starts
        reading as less serious than it is.
        Hovering the disc shows the RAILWAY remark on an approved alert and the
        DISCARD remark on a discarded one -- different fields, because they answer
        different questions: what the maintainer found in the field, versus why the
        alert was thrown away. Hovering the star chip on the OPPOSITE edge shows the
        AI-analysis rating and the reviewer's remark about it.
        Both flyouts open on keyboard focus as well as hover, close on Escape, scroll
        and resize, and are position:fixed on <body>
    -- drawn inside a card, the grid
    and the day sections would clip them. When there is no room on the preferred
    side they drop BELOW the anchor rather than flipping across it, because a
    flipped popover lands on top of the very card it is describing.
    The AI rating is DISPLAY ONLY. Nothing writes it yet, deliberately: 1.0.55.101
    removed the validity buttons because they posted to an endpoint that no longer
    existed, and a control that cannot save is worse than no control -- the
    operator believes a judgement was recorded when it was not.
    Table and Timeline are carried over unchanged and still reachable from the
    view toggle; both gained the disc and the rating chip for parity.
    The site tiles, the type segment and the three counters follow the review
    state. A tile reading "1 failure" above a list showing nothing, because that
    failure was discarded, is a counter arguing with the list under it.

