    1.0.55.85 - TIMELINE REBUILT to the owner's sketch: one day per row, cards flowing left to right,
    each with a stem down to its node on a time axis, its time printed beneath.
    The axis is SEQUENTIAL, not proportional -- and that single choice is what ends four
    revisions of collision fixing. Positioning cards at a true time coordinate on a 24 h
    scale meant alerts minutes apart could never sit side by side, so the lane could only
    stack, spill or hide them. In flow, two alerts a minute apart are simply two adjacent
    cards. Real times stay on the axis labels; a burst (same site, within 20 min) collapses
    to one card with its count, so 14 Aug is 13 cards rather than 32.
    Chronological within a day, newest day first.
    1.0.55.84 - The lanes read as a diagonal staircase instead of a horizontal timeline. The collision
    threshold was 0.045 of the lane -- 108px, or 65 MINUTES -- while a chip like "2T" is
    about 34px, roughly 20 minutes. So every alert in the same HOUR was pushed to its own
    row and the result cascaded downward. Threshold is now derived from the actual chip
    width against the measured lane width. And a genuine BURST -- 3+ alerts inside one chip
    width -- collapses into ONE cluster chip carrying its count, because at true position
    they physically overlap and no layout can spread them. On the 14 Aug data 16 alerts now
    render as 9 marks on ONE row instead of a 6-deep staircase.
